IMPORT prelude

-- Corporate Bodies' Contracts Act 1960 (Singapore) -- how a body corporate may make, vary and discharge a contract without a
-- seal (s 1), who such a contract binds (s 1(2)), and which bodies the Act does not reach (s 2).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CBCA1960.txt. The deposit says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the latest amendment annotated is
-- S 26/2022 (w.e.f. 13 January 2022), which added variable capital companies to the s 2 exclusion.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0116 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by everyday-life
-- relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper question yet.
-- The Act has three sections, and this row encodes all of its operative text: ss 1 and 2 (s 3 is the short title).
--
-- Not encoded: what "a contract which if made between private persons would by law be required to be in writing" covers -- that is
-- set by other law (no such law was retrieved); the law of authority (whether a signatory in fact had express or implied authority);
-- the formal requirements of a contract under seal, which s 1(4) preserves but does not regulate; and the old s 5 of the Civil Law
-- Act 1909, which s 1(5) refers to for pre-12 November 1993 contracts but which is not in the deposit.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use. See NOTES.md.

§ `Corporate Bodies' Contracts Act 1960 -- nouns`

-- Illustrative bodies. Whether a particular statutory body is a "body corporate" is set by its own Act, which was not retrieved;
-- `a statutory body corporate` stands for one that is.
DECLARE Body IS ONE OF
    `a statutory body corporate`
    `a body corporate incorporated outside Singapore`
    `a company under the Companies Act 1967`
    `a limited liability partnership`
    `a variable capital company`

-- s 1(1) sorts contracts by what the law would require "if made between private persons". A deed is our example of a form that
-- neither (a) nor (b) describes.
DECLARE Formality IS ONE OF
    `writing signed by the party to be charged`
    `valid by parol`
    `a deed`

DECLARE Authority IS ONE OF
    `express authority`
    `implied authority`
    `no authority`

DECLARE Party IS ONE OF
    `the body corporate`
    `its successors`
    `the other parties to the contract`
    `the person who signed on its behalf`

§§ `2 -- bodies the Act does not reach`

-- s 2: "This Act shall not apply to any company as defined in the Companies Act 1967, any limited liability partnership as defined
-- in the Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2005 or any variable capital company as defined in the Variable Capital Companies Act
-- 2018." [5/2005] [S 26/2022 wef 13/01/2022]
-- s 1(1): "any body corporate, wherever incorporated".
-- A foreign body corporate is treated as within the Act. Whether a foreign company registered in Singapore is a "company as defined
-- in the Companies Act 1967" depends on that Act's definition, which was not retrieved: an INFERENCE that it is not.
GIVEN `the body` IS A Body
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies to the body` IF
    CONSIDER `the body`
    WHEN `a company under the Companies Act 1967` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a limited liability partnership` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a variable capital company` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `1(1) -- making a contract without a seal`

-- s 1(1)(a): a contract which between private persons "would by law be required to be in writing, signed by the parties to be
-- charged therewith, may be made on behalf of the body corporate in writing signed by any person acting under its authority,
-- express or implied".
-- s 1(1)(b): a contract which between private persons "would by law be valid although made by parol only, and not reduced into
-- writing, may be made by parol on behalf of the body corporate by any person acting under its authority, express or implied".
GIVEN `the authority` IS AN Authority
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person acted under its authority` IF
    CONSIDER `the authority`
    WHEN `no authority` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- Reading: s 1(1) covers only the (a) and (b) kinds. A contract that between private persons would need some other form (a deed,
-- say) gets no route from s 1; s 1(4) leaves the seal open. A parol-valid contract may of course also be made in writing.
GIVEN `the formality` IS A Formality, `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN, `signed by that person` IS A BOOLEAN, `the authority` IS AN Authority
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the form satisfies s 1(1)` IF
        `the person acted under its authority` `the authority`
    AND CONSIDER `the formality`
        WHEN `writing signed by the party to be charged` THEN `in writing` AND `signed by that person`
        WHEN `valid by parol` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `1(2), 1(5) -- effectual and binding`

-- s 1(2): "A contract made according to this section shall be effectual in law, and shall bind the body corporate and its
-- successors and all other parties thereto."
-- s 1(5): "Nothing in this section applies to the making, variation or discharge of a contract before 12 November 1993 except in so
-- far as it was applicable by virtue of section 5 of the Civil Law Act 1909 in force before that date." The old s 5 is not in the
-- deposit, so whether it applied is taken as a bare fact.
GIVEN `the body` IS A Body, `the formality` IS A Formality, `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN, `signed by that person` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the authority` IS AN Authority, `made on or after 12 November 1993` IS A BOOLEAN, `the old Civil Law Act s 5 applied` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 1 makes the contract effectual` IF
        `the Act applies to the body` `the body`
    AND `the form satisfies s 1(1)` `the formality` `in writing` `signed by that person` `the authority`
    AND (`made on or after 12 November 1993` OR `the old Civil Law Act s 5 applied`)

-- s 1(2) names three: the body corporate, its successors, and all other parties. It says nothing about the person who signed; this
-- rule answers only whom s 1(2) names, not whether the signatory is liable under other law.
GIVEN `the party` IS A Party
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 1(2) names the party as bound` IF
    CONSIDER `the party`
    WHEN `the person who signed on its behalf` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `1(3), 1(4) -- variation, discharge and the seal`

-- s 1(3): "A contract made according to this section may be varied or discharged in the same manner in which it is authorised by
-- this section to be made." So a variation of an (a)-kind contract needs writing signed under authority; a (b)-kind may be varied
-- by parol.
-- s 1(4): "Nothing in this section shall be taken as preventing a contract under seal from being made by or on behalf of a body
-- corporate." The seal is an alternative, not a requirement; its own formalities are not encoded.
GIVEN `the body` IS A Body, `the formality` IS A Formality, `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN, `signed by that person` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the authority` IS AN Authority, `made on or after 12 November 1993` IS A BOOLEAN, `the old Civil Law Act s 5 applied` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the variation or discharge is effective under s 1(3)` IF
    `s 1 makes the contract effectual` `the body` `the formality` `in writing` `signed by that person` `the authority` `made on or after 12 November 1993` `the old Civil Law Act s 5 applied`
